    gameId: '7-toed-pete-dominoes',
    icon: '🃏',
  },
  data: {
    LLM: 'The game uses a double-six domino set. Each player receives seven tiles. In the four-player game there is no boneyard; in the two- or three-player variant, remaining tiles form a boneyard to draw from when a player cannot play. The first scoring tile or double led starts the layout, and the first double played becomes the only spinner, from which four arms of a cross-shaped layout grow. Players in turn match the open ends; whenever a player makes the open ends total a multiple of five or plays a double, that player takes an immediate extra turn. If a player cannot play and there is a boneyard, they must draw until able to play; otherwise they pass. A hand ends when a player dominoes with their last tile or when the game is blocked with no legal plays.',
    Player: 'The game uses a double-six domino set. Each player receives seven tiles. In the four-player game there is no boneyard; in the two- or three-player variant, remaining tiles form a boneyard to draw from when a player cannot play. The first scoring tile or double led starts the layout, and the first double played becomes the only spinner, from which four arms of a cross-shaped layout grow. Players in turn match the open ends; whenever a player makes the open ends total a multiple of five or plays a double, that player takes an immediate extra turn. If a player cannot play and there is a boneyard, they must draw until able to play; otherwise they pass. A hand ends when a player dominoes with their last tile or when the game is blocked with no legal plays.',
    objective: 'Score points by making the open ends of the layout total a multiple of five and by winning hands with the lowest pip total; the first player to reach the agreed total of points or chips wins.',
    gameplay: 'The game uses a double-six domino set. Each player receives seven tiles. In the four-player game there is no boneyard; in the two- or three-player variant, remaining tiles form a boneyard to draw from when a player cannot play. The first scoring tile or double led starts the layout, and the first double played becomes the only spinner, from which four arms of a cross-shaped layout grow. Players in turn match the open ends; whenever a player makes the open ends total a multiple of five or plays a double, that player takes an immediate extra turn. If a player cannot play and there is a boneyard, they must draw until able to play; otherwise they pass. A hand ends when a player dominoes with their last tile or when the game is blocked with no legal plays.',
    keyRules: [
      'Member of the Fives family of domino games; scoring on multiples of five.',
      'Uses a double-six domino set and usually four players, each taking seven tiles.',
      'Only the first double played becomes the spinner, producing a four-armed cross layout.',
      'Playing a double or making the open ends total a multiple of five grants an extra turn.',
      "A hand ends when a player dominoes or the game is blocked; the winner scores from opponents' remaining tiles.",
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: false,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
    setup: {
      players: 'Four players in the basic game; two or three players can play a draw variant with a boneyard.',
      deck: 'Double-six domino set (28 tiles)',
      equipment: 'Double-six domino set, scoring chips or markers',
      dealing: 'For four players, each receives seven tiles and there is no boneyard. For two or three players, each receives seven tiles and the remaining tiles form a boneyard to draw from when unable to play.',
    },
    turnFlow: 'The game uses a double-six domino set. Each player receives seven tiles. In the four-player game there is no boneyard; in the two- or three-player variant, remaining tiles form a boneyard to draw from when a player cannot play. The first scoring tile or double led starts the layout, and the first double played becomes the only spinner, from which four arms of a cross-shaped layout grow. Players in turn match the open ends; whenever a player makes the open ends total a multiple of five or plays a double, that player takes an immediate extra turn. If a player cannot play and there is a boneyard, they must draw until able to play; otherwise they pass. A hand ends when a player dominoes with their last tile or when the game is blocked with no legal plays.',
