    exampleHands: [
      'Setup example: 7 card(s) per player.',
      'Rule example: Score points by making the open ends of the layout total a multiple of five and by winning hands with the lowest pip total; the first player to reach the agreed total of points or chips wins.',
      'Turn-flow example: The game uses a double-six domino set. Each player receives seven tiles. In the four-player game there is no boneyard; in the two- or three-player variant, remaining tiles form a boneyard to draw from when a player cannot play. The first scoring tile or double led starts the layout, and the first double played becomes the only spinner, from which four arms of a cross-shaped layout grow. Players in turn match the open ends; whenever a player makes the open ends total a multiple of five or plays a double, that player takes an immediate extra turn. If a player cannot play and there is a boneyard, they must draw until able to play; otherwise they pass. A hand ends when a player dominoes with their last tile or when the game is blocked with no legal plays.',
      "Scoring example: Whenever a player makes the open ends of the layout total a multiple of five, they score that total; the winner of the hand (the player who dominoes or has the fewest pips when the game blocks) scores the total pip value of opponents' remaining tiles, often divided by five into points or chips. Play continues over multiple hands, and the first player to reach the agreed total of points or chips wins..",
